# Instant site updates

Writing a file is not done until the running site shows that change. Finish in the same turn.

## Before you finish

1. Confirm the `dev` server compiled the edit (terminal or `.next/dev/logs/next-development.log`). A syntax error means the page is still the old version — fix it before saying the change is done.
2. After DB / JsonStore / delivery / category / upload changes, `POST http://localhost:3000/api/dev/refresh` so TTL caches and `revalidatePath` drop immediately (do not wait for the 15s search-airports TTL).
3. Open the affected route in the browser and confirm the new behavior. Hard-refresh if the document looks stale.
4. When layout or UI changed, check the same screen at phone width (~390px).
5. `next start` serves the last production build. Source edits do not appear there until a new build and restart. Use `next dev` for live edits. Do not rebuild or restart production unless the user asked to deploy.

## Saves that change public content

- On write, store the new value in the in-memory cache (`cache.set`) and call `revalidatePublishedContent()` so the next request is the new content.
- Do not put a long `Cache-Control` max-age on HTML or on JSON the public site must refresh. Use `no-store` for those responses.
- Keep working against `next dev` on localhost so HMR + `/api/dev/refresh` show every change in the same turn.
